<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congo Basin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congo basin is the vast drainage area of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>, largely covered by tropical rainforest. Its southern savanna fringes were the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> states. The Lunda Empire's trade networks extended across this region, linking the interior with Atlantic commerce.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congo-basin.jpg" alt="Congo Basin" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congobasinmap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its people, the Luyi or Aluyana, later known as the Lozi, probably came from the north, and their own traditions link them to the Lunda and Luba world of the southern <b>Congo basin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Berlin Conference of 1884–1885 the association signed agreements with the powers recognising its control of most of the <b>Congo basin</b>, and the conference’s General Act of 26 February 1885 guaranteed free trade and navigation there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The state's growth was helped by fertile river valleys and by its position between the copper-producing lands to the south-east and the trade routes of the <b>Congo basin</b> and Angola.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Caravans organised by Arab, Swahili and Nyamwezi traders and financed by Indian merchants travelled inland to Tabora, Ujiji on Lake Tanganyika and beyond, returning with ivory and captives; Tippu Tip, the most famous of these traders, built a personal domain in the eastern <b>Congo basin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Bemba tradition traces the royal Crocodile clan to Luba lands in the <b>Congo basin</b>, from which its founders migrated east and settled on the Lubemba plateau.</blockquote><a href="/state/bemba-kingdom">Bemba Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>According to tradition, the Phiri clan migrated from the Luba lands of the <b>Congo basin</b> and settled south-west of Lake Malawi in the late 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-maravi">Kingdom of Maravi</a></li></ul></section>
